WEBVTT

﻿1
00:00:00.895 --> 00:00:06.039
TouchHub retargets human glove touch into the native tactile channels of robot hands.

2
00:00:06.039 --> 00:00:12.316
Sharpa and XHand use these human demonstrations for three contact-rich manipulation tasks.

3
00:00:13.495 --> 00:00:18.195
Human demonstrations are becoming an important source of data for robot learning.

4
00:00:18.195 --> 00:00:23.048
As these examples show, researchers collect touch using many different gloves.

5
00:00:23.048 --> 00:00:27.730
But their sensing principles, taxel layouts, and surface coverage differ.

6
00:00:27.730 --> 00:00:33.700
These differences make direct reuse difficult and motivate the related work we consider next.

7
00:00:35.695 --> 00:00:38.918
We first compare the closest prior approaches.

8
00:00:38.918 --> 00:00:42.373
FTP-1 uses sensor-specific encoders.

9
00:00:42.373 --> 00:00:46.941
TACROSS unifies region-level semantics rather than native taxels.

10
00:00:46.941 --> 00:00:50.305
UniTacHand needs paired human and robot touch.

11
00:00:50.305 --> 00:00:54.077
TactAlign derives pseudo-pairs from egocentric poses.

12
00:00:54.077 --> 00:00:59.980
However, TouchHub reconstructs native readings from layouts and unlabeled recordings.

13
00:01:01.095 --> 00:01:06.216
We now introduce TouchHub across different sensing technologies and coverage patterns.

14
00:01:06.216 --> 00:01:12.372
Registering their taxel layouts on a shared MANO surface provides a common geometric reference.

15
00:01:13.495 --> 00:01:18.195
The shared surface connects heterogeneous gloves to dexterous robot hands.

16
00:01:18.195 --> 00:01:24.100
TouchHub translates their recordings directly into each target hand's native tactile channels.

17
00:01:25.895 --> 00:01:29.243
Here, a Matrix press is transferred to Crocket.

18
00:01:29.243 --> 00:01:34.289
Geometric transport spreads the compact contact across too many taxels.

19
00:01:34.289 --> 00:01:40.124
TouchHub restores the footprint and calibrates its intensity toward the measured target response.

20
00:01:41.229 --> 00:01:45.815
A fixed cutoff can erase light touches and the weaker contact in a pinch.

21
00:01:45.815 --> 00:01:49.111
In bulb screwing, the ninetieth-percentile contact total is

22
00:01:49.111 --> 00:01:52.486
two point seven eight times measured XHand with linear gain,

23
00:01:52.486 --> 00:01:55.937
versus one point zero nine with TouchHub.

24
00:02:04.479 --> 00:02:09.486
Geometry projects source contact, then four Euler steps restore its footprint.

25
00:02:09.486 --> 00:02:14.088
A new target provides only its 3D layout and unlabeled recordings.

26
00:02:14.088 --> 00:02:16.895
Their statistics calibrate intensity.

27
00:02:16.895 --> 00:02:22.187
Local attention links nearby taxels before reconstructing native readings.

28
00:02:26.129 --> 00:02:31.511
In this held-out palm press, TouchHub concentrates activation near the measured contact.

29
00:02:31.511 --> 00:02:35.941
The bars compare centroid error and off-region activation.

30
00:02:37.062 --> 00:02:42.148
The thumb example shows the same goal: a compact footprint at the correct location.

31
00:02:42.148 --> 00:02:47.787
TouchHub reduces extra responses without cross-sensor paired contacts for training.

32
00:02:48.895 --> 00:02:54.720
Across twelve transfer directions, TouchHub reduces log-reading error to zero point six.

33
00:02:54.720 --> 00:03:00.311
That is about thirty percent below OpenTac-style, the strongest baseline for this metric.

34
00:03:00.311 --> 00:03:03.916
It also achieves the lowest false activation.

35
00:03:05.029 --> 00:03:11.263
Unseen sensors provide three-dimensional layouts and unlabeled touch, while both networks stay frozen.

36
00:03:11.263 --> 00:03:15.206
With both Matrix skins held out, TouchHub reduces false activation

37
00:03:15.206 --> 00:03:18.854
by eighty-nine percent compared with geometric transport.

38
00:03:18.854 --> 00:03:22.641
New devices also show consistent reductions.

39
00:03:23.762 --> 00:03:27.939
TouchHub reaches ninety percent success on XHand bulb screwing.

40
00:03:27.939 --> 00:03:34.895
Across the target gloves, it achieves forty-eight point one percent average first-contact finger accuracy.

41
00:03:40.262 --> 00:03:44.308
On Sharpa, bulb success rises from forty percent with robot-only training

42
00:03:44.308 --> 00:03:46.803
to ninety-three percent with TouchHub.

43
00:03:46.803 --> 00:03:51.755
The masked baseline removes human touch, while retaining robot touch.

44
00:04:00.262 --> 00:04:03.876
On XHand, full-budget success is ninety percent for bulbs,

45
00:04:03.876 --> 00:04:07.678
one hundred percent for cups, and ninety percent for wiping.

46
00:04:07.678 --> 00:04:12.403
TouchHub outperforms both baselines across all three tasks.

47
00:04:20.262 --> 00:04:25.292
Removing either restoration or scale calibration increases false activation.

48
00:04:25.292 --> 00:04:28.542
Synthetic intermediate MANO layouts achieve comparable results

49
00:04:28.542 --> 00:04:32.280
without data from other real skins for restoration training.

50
00:04:32.280 --> 00:04:36.291
Real within-sensor touch recordings are still used.

51
00:04:37.395 --> 00:04:40.668
TouchHub makes human touch reusable across robot hands:

52
00:04:40.668 --> 00:04:44.168
native-taxel retargeting, frozen-model sensor onboarding,

53
00:04:44.168 --> 00:04:48.672
and effective human-to-robot co-training with limited robot data.
